<v Ira Glass>You're not in touch with the whole scene, yeah.
<v Ira Glass>你不怎么接触那个圈子，是吧。

00:19:03.650 --> 00:19:31.120
<v Duki>When I had time, I used to migrate once in a while, because I had some friends who are ducks. And I try to keep in touch with them. But lately, I've just started spending more time with people and doing my own thing. And I just don't have time to do those duck things anymore. I just wanted more in my life than that.
<v Duki>以前我有时间的时候，偶尔会迁徙，因为我有一些鸭子朋友。我也尽量和他们保持联系。但最近，我开始花更多时间和人在一起，做我自己的事。我只是没时间再去做那些鸭子的事了。我的生活里想要更多不仅仅是那些东西。

00:19:31.120 --> 00:20:29.430
<v Duki>[MUSIC PLAYING - "SATURDAY NIGHT FISH FRY" BY LOUIS JORDAN]
<v Duki>[播放音乐 - 路易斯·乔丹的《周六夜鱼炸饼》]

00:20:29.430 --> 00:20:55.390
<v Ira Glass>Well, the story of a 26-year-old graduate student who talks like a duck brings us naturally to the subject of Chickenman. Chickenman first soared the radio airwaves from 1966 to 1969. And nearly every day, there would be a new episode for three years. These are short, little two-minute things, starting on WCFL here in our beloved Chicago.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，一个26岁的研究生像鸭子一样说话的故事，自然地引出了我们关于“鸡侠”（Chickenman）的主题。鸡侠从1966年到1969年首次在广播电台腾飞。三年来，几乎每天都有新的一集。这些都是两分钟的小短剧，最初是在我们心爱的芝加哥WCFL电台播出的。

00:20:55.390 --> 00:21:16.390
<v Ira Glass>And after that, Chickenman appeared on over 1,500 radio stations across the country, around the world. According to the people who syndicate it these are the numbers. They say it's been translated into German, Dutch, and Swedish. It is still on the air, they say, in several dozen markets, making it one of the longest-running radio features anywhere.
<v Ira Glass>此后，鸡侠出现在全国乃至世界各地的1500多家广播电台。据发行它的公司统计，这就是数据。他们说它已经被翻译成德语、荷兰语和瑞典语。据说它至今仍在几十个市场播出，使其成为史上最长寿的广播节目之一。

00:21:16.390 --> 00:21:40.920
<v Ira Glass>Chickenman began years before National Public Radio existed. It will probably continue years after we are gone. Like the mighty cockroach, like, I don't know, the bagel, like Hamlet, Chickenman endures, will endure.
<v Ira Glass>鸡侠在全国公共广播电台存在之前就开始了。它很可能在我们离开多年后还会继续。就像强大的蟑螂，就像……不知道，就像贝果，就像哈姆雷特，鸡侠永存，并将继续存在。

00:21:40.920 --> 00:22:06.790
<v Ira Glass>Coming up later in the show, we'll have the first new Chickenman episode since 1969. Have we mentioned this enough in the show? Should we just mention it every time? Every time we open the mike, I should just mention that? It was written especially for our broadcast by Dick Orkin, the voice of Chickenman. But before we hear that, let's just hear what all the fuss was about.
<v Ira Glass>节目稍后，我们将播出自1969年以来的第一集新版《鸡侠》。我们在节目里提到得够多了吗？我是不是应该每次都提一下？每次我们打开麦克风，我就应该提一下吗？它是为我们的广播特别创作的，作者是迪克·奥金（Dick Orkin），也就是鸡侠的配音。但在听那个之前，让我们先听听大家都在兴奋什么。

00:22:06.790 --> 00:22:14.330
<v Chickenman Announcer>Now, another exciting episode in the life of the most fantastic crimefighter the world has ever known.
<v Chickenman Announcer>现在，全世界有史以来最伟大的打击犯罪英雄生活中的又一个激动人心的篇章。

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:18.590
<v Chickenman>Bock bock bock bock!
<v Chickenman>咯咯咯咯！

00:22:18.590 --> 00:22:19.580
<v Man 1>Chickenman!
<v Man 1>鸡侠！

00:22:19.580 --> 00:22:24.040
<v Women>He's everywhere, he's everywhere!
<v Women>他无处不在，他无处不在！

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:48.660
<v Chickenman Announcer>Benton Harbor, employed as a shoe salesman for a large downtown department store, spends his weekends, his only two days off, striking terror into the hearts of criminals everywhere as the white-winged warrior called Chickenman. How did it come about that Benton Harbor, weekend winged warrior, selected the visage of the chicken in his crusade against the forces of evil? Now it can be told.
<v Chickenman Announcer>本顿·哈伯（Benton Harbor），市中心一家大型百货公司的鞋类推销员，利用他仅有的周末两天假期，化身为名为“鸡侠”的白翼战士，让各地的罪犯心惊胆战。本顿·哈伯这个周末的带翼战士，是如何在打击邪恶势力的斗争中选择鸡的形象的？现在可以揭晓了。

