<v Woman>嗯，您心里有什么想法吗？

00:22:53.950 --> 00:22:57.010
<v Benton Harbor>Something that will strike terror into the hearts of criminals everywhere.
<v Benton Harbor>某种能让各地罪犯闻风丧胆的服装。

00:22:57.010 --> 00:22:58.740
<v Woman>I see. Well, how about this?
<v Woman>我明白了。嗯，这件怎么样？

00:22:58.740 --> 00:23:00.510
<v Benton Harbor>Hmm. No, I don't think so.
<v Benton Harbor>嗯，我觉得不太行。

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:01.390
<v Woman>Why not try it on?
<v Woman>为什么不试试看呢？

00:23:01.390 --> 00:23:02.290
<v Benton Harbor>Very well.
<v Benton Harbor>好吧。

00:23:02.290 --> 00:23:03.240
<v Woman>Here, I'll help you.
<v Woman>来，我帮您。

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:04.700
<v Benton Harbor>Thank you.
<v Benton Harbor>谢谢。

00:23:04.700 --> 00:23:06.690
<v Woman>There you are. Now take a look in the mirror.
<v Woman>好了。现在照照镜子看看。

00:23:06.690 --> 00:23:11.780
<v Benton Harbor>Hmm. Not bad. I wonder if you would permit me to conduct a quick experiment outside this store.
<v Benton Harbor>嗯。还不错。不知道您是否允许我在店外进行一个小实验。

00:23:11.780 --> 00:23:19.570
<v Woman>Certainly.
<v Woman>当然。

00:23:19.570 --> 00:23:20.840
<v Benton Harbor>Pardon me, sir.
<v Benton Harbor>请原谅，先生。

00:23:20.840 --> 00:23:21.190
<v Criminal>Yeah?
<v Criminal>什么事？

00:23:21.190 --> 00:23:23.420
<v Benton Harbor>Are you by chance a vicious criminal?
<v Benton Harbor>请问你是一个穷凶极恶的罪犯吗？

00:23:23.420 --> 00:23:24.180
<v Criminal>Uh-huh.
<v Criminal>嗯，算是吧。

00:23:24.180 --> 00:23:26.280
<v Benton Harbor>Fine. Would you take a look at this costume I'm wearing?
<v Benton Harbor>好吧。你能看看我身上穿的这套戏服吗？

00:23:26.280 --> 00:23:27.030
<v Criminal>Yeah.
<v Criminal>嗯。

00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:28.560
<v Benton Harbor>Do you feel anything strange?
<v Benton Harbor>你有什么奇怪的感觉吗？

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:29.430
<v Criminal>Uh--
<v Criminal>呃——

00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:31.160
<v Benton Harbor>Anything at all?
<v Benton Harbor>有什么感觉吗？

00:23:31.160 --> 00:23:31.990
<v Criminal>Uh, yeah.
<v Criminal>呃，有。

00:23:31.990 --> 00:23:33.370
<v Benton Harbor>And what is that?
<v Benton Harbor>那是什么感觉？

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:35.900
<v Criminal>I'd like to kiss you.
<v Criminal>我想吻你。

00:23:35.900 --> 00:23:36.750
<v Benton Harbor>Kiss me?
<v Benton Harbor>吻我？

00:23:36.750 --> 00:23:37.500
<v Criminal>Yeah.
<v Criminal>对。

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:38.780
<v Benton Harbor>How do you account for that?
<v Benton Harbor>你怎么解释这种感觉？

00:23:38.780 --> 00:23:46.680
<v Criminal>Because you look like an adorable bunny rabbit.
<v Criminal>因为你看上去就像一只可爱的兔宝宝。

00:23:46.680 --> 00:23:48.820
<v Woman>Well, how did it go?
<v Woman>嗯，进行得怎么样了？

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:50.170
<v Benton Harbor>What else do you have?
<v Benton Harbor>你还有什么别的？

00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:52.280
<v Woman>A Teddy bear and a chicken.
<v Woman>一只泰迪熊和一只小鸡。

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:53.440
<v Benton Harbor>A Teddy bear?
<v Benton Harbor>一只泰迪熊？

00:23:53.440 --> 00:23:54.590
<v Woman>It'd be cute.
<v Woman>那会很可爱。

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:56.680
<v Benton Harbor>Wrap up the chicken, please.
<v Benton Harbor>请把小鸡包起来。

00:23:56.680 --> 00:24:05.580
<v Chickenman Announcer>Be listening tomorrow for another exciting episode in the life of the most fantastic crimefighter the world has ever known.
<v Chickenman Announcer>请明天继续收听世界上最了不起的打击犯罪英雄的另一段激动人心的生活。

00:24:05.580 --> 00:24:09.420
<v Chickenman>Bock bock bock bock!
<v Chickenman>咯咯咯咯！

00:24:09.420 --> 00:24:11.340
<v Man 2>Chickenman!
<v Man 2>鸡侠！

00:24:11.340 --> 00:24:17.110
<v Women>He's everywhere, he's everywhere!
<v Women>他无处不在，他无处不在！

00:24:17.110 --> 00:25:20.320
<v Ira Glass>The all-new Chickenman, coming up at the end of our show. But next, a real-life story of real poultry and a real poultry farm. Stay with us, won't you?
<v Ira Glass>全新的鸡侠，将在我们节目结束时播出。但接下来，是一个关于真实家禽和真实养鸡场的真实故事。请继续关注我们，好吗？

00:25:20.320 --> 00:25:36.940
<v Julie Showalter>I'm Julie Showalter. I grew up on a turkey farm in southwest Missouri. "The night 3,000 turkeys died." The day before the night that 3,000 turkeys died, we moved 13,000 turkeys to the range.
<v Julie Showalter>我是朱莉·肖沃尔特。我在密苏里州西南部的一个火鸡养殖场长大。“三千只火鸡死掉的那晚”。在那三千只火鸡死掉的那晚的前一天，我们将一万三千只火鸡转移到了放养区。

00:25:36.940 --> 00:25:48.910
<v Julie Showalter>This requires some explanation. Turkeys spend their first 16 weeks in a heated brooder house. When they are 16 weeks old, they are put outside to range in fenced enclosures.
<v Julie Showalter>这需要一些解释。火鸡前16周是在加热的育雏舍中度过的。当它们长到16周大时，它们会被放到户外带围栏的封闭区域放养。

00:25:48.910 --> 00:26:09.750
<v Julie Showalter>Daddy decided we would herd them to the range. It looked simple enough. We made a temporary chute of wire fencing that ran from the double-end doors of the brooder house 50 yards to the pen. We would get behind the turkeys in the brooder house, shout, wave old shirts and gunny sacks at them, and they would run out the doors, through the chute, into the pen.
<v Julie Showalter>爸爸决定让我们把它们赶到放养区。这看起来很简单。我们用铁丝网围成了一条临时的通道，从育雏舍的双开门一直延伸到50码外的围栏里。我们站在育雏舍里的火鸡后面，大喊大叫，挥舞着旧衬衫和麻袋，它们就会从门里跑出来，穿过通道，进入围栏。

00:26:09.750 --> 00:26:29.510
<v Julie Showalter>And that's the way it worked in the first brooder house. The first turkeys hesitated at the door, walked out cautiously, then moved through the chute and dispersed. The rest followed. It took about an hour. Daddy was pleased. "Let's work straight through," he said. "We'll be done by 10:00."
<v Julie Showalter>这就是在第一个育雏舍里的工作方式。第一批火鸡在门口犹豫了一下，小心翼翼地走出来，然后穿过通道散开了。其余的火鸡紧随其后。大约花了一个小时。爸爸很高兴。“我们一鼓作气干完吧，”他说，“10点钟就能完工。”

00:26:29.510 --> 00:26:43.360
<v Julie Showalter>By the time the turkeys have been in a brooder house for 16 weeks, the air is filled with ammonia, feather particles, and dust. The stench is overwhelming. After an hour in the brooder house, your lungs hurt for a day.
<v Julie Showalter>当火鸡在育雏舍待满16周时，空气中就充满了氨气、羽毛微粒和灰尘。那股恶臭令人窒息。在育雏舍待上一个小时，你的肺就会疼上一整天。

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:57.090
<v Julie Showalter>You can contract disabling lung diseases from working only a week in a poultry house. Tiny barbed pieces of feather dig into the tissue of your lung and never let go. But we didn't know that then.
<v Julie Showalter>在禽舍工作仅仅一周，你就可能染上严重的肺病。细小的带倒钩的羽毛碎片会扎进肺部组织，永远无法排出。但当时我们并不知道这一点。

00:26:57.090 --> 00:27:11.140
<v Julie Showalter>We moved the temporary fence to the doors of the second brooder house. When we threw open the doors at the end of the second house, it was 9:00 in the morning. The sun streamed in the open doors on turkeys that had never seen direct sunlight.
<v Julie Showalter>我们把临时栅栏移到了第二个育雏舍的门前。当我们打开第二个舍房的门时，是上午9点。阳光照进了敞开的门，照在了那些从未见过直射阳光的火鸡身上。

00:27:11.140 --> 00:27:29.670
<v Julie Showalter>The one thing you can count on with turkeys is that you never know how they are going to react. I've seen turkeys clamber against a fence trying to get into a range fire. I've seen them rush toward a screaming child, trying to kill it. And I've seen them run from a screaming child, spooked and terrified.
<v Julie Showalter>关于火鸡，你唯一能确定的是你永远不知道它们会作何反应。我见过火鸡挤在栅栏上试图冲进放养区的火火里。我见过它们冲向尖叫的孩子，试图杀死它。我也见过它们被尖叫的孩子吓得四散奔逃，惊恐万状。

00:27:29.670 --> 00:27:44.230
<v Julie Showalter>These turkeys didn't want to go into the sun. As we pushed from behind, they compacted. It was like an old adventure movie where the walls are closing in. But there was no wall at the end, only a patch of sunlight which turkeys would not touch.
<v Julie Showalter>这些火鸡不想进入阳光下。当我们从后面推它们时，它们挤在了一起。就像那种墙壁正在闭合的旧冒险电影一样。但尽头没有墙，只有一片火鸡绝不肯踏入的阳光区域。

00:27:44.230 --> 00:28:02.790
<v Julie Showalter>We yelled louder, waved our cloths, kicked at the ones in the rear. Finally, Daddy walked through the solid carpet of turkeys to break the logjam at the front. He stood at the edge of the sunlight, lifting the turkeys three or four at a time with his feet, stirring them with this legs, forcing them into the sun.
<v Julie Showalter>我们大声呼喊，挥动手中的布料，踢着后面的火鸡。最后，爸爸穿过那片厚实的火鸡群，试图打破前面的拥堵。他站在阳光的边缘，用脚一次次把三四只火鸡拨开，用腿搅动它们，强迫它们进入阳光下。

00:28:02.790 --> 00:28:27.320
<v Julie Showalter>Suddenly, they broke free. As stubbornly as they had refused to go into the light, they now rushed toward it. They ran in a panic, piling on top of each other, knocking down the temporary fence. By the time Daddy could get the doors closed, at least 1,000 turkeys had escaped and were running free on the farm, onto our neighbor's farm, into the road.
<v Julie Showalter>突然，它们冲破了阻碍。它们刚才有多固执地拒绝进入光亮处，现在就有多狂乱地冲向那里。它们惊慌失措地奔跑，堆叠在一起，撞倒了临时栅栏。等爸爸赶去把门关上时，至少有1000只火鸡逃了出去，在农场里、邻居的农场里以及公路上四处乱跑。

00:28:27.320 --> 00:28:42.970
<v Julie Showalter>We didn't own the turkeys. We raised them for a company that owned the hatchery, the feed mill, the fleet of trucks that delivered and loaded the turkeys, the processing plant. We got a portion of the profits, if there were profits.
<v Julie Showalter>这些火鸡不属于我们。我们是为一家公司代养的，这家公司拥有孵化场、饲料厂、运送和装载火鸡的卡车队，以及加工厂。我们只能分到一部分利润，前提是必须有利润。

00:28:42.970 --> 00:29:04.670
<v Julie Showalter>With 1,000 turkeys gone, there would be no profits on this flock. 16 weeks of Daddy working 14-hour days, of my sisters and me working alongside him any time we weren't in school, all for no pay. And if we weren't paid for this flock, we would have no cash coming in until the next flock was raised.
<v Julie Showalter>丢了1000只火鸡，这批火鸡就不会有利润了。爸爸16周以来每天工作14个小时，我和姐妹们只要不在上学就跟他一起干，所有这些都是白干。如果我们这批火鸡拿不到报酬，在下一批火鸡养大之前，我们将没有任何现金收入。

00:29:04.670 --> 00:29:30.240
<v Julie Showalter>It took us eight hours to round up the escaped turkeys, four of us trying to track down 1,000 birds that had the whole world in which to hide and run from us. The sun beat down, and the air was thick and humid. We stopped once for water, and my sister, Billie, the youngest of us, just 11, vomited from the cold water hitting her stomach after hours of sun, heat, and dehydration.
<v Julie Showalter>我们花了八个小时才找回那些逃跑的火鸡，我们四个人试图追踪1000只鸟，而它们有整个世界可以用来躲藏和奔跑。烈日当空，空气又厚又湿。我们停下来喝了一次水，我最小的妹妹比莉，才11岁，因为在阳光、高温和脱水下待了几个小时后喝了冷水，竟然呕吐了。

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:48.360
<v Julie Showalter>As she lay on the ground, shaking and holding her stomach, I hated her for being the one too sick to continue. But even she was not too sick. We all went on. She got an extra five minutes to rest, but we all went on.
<v Julie Showalter>当她躺在地上，颤抖着捂住肚子时，我恨她因为生病而无法继续。但即使是她，也没有病到无法坚持的地步。我们都继续前行。她多休息了五分钟，但我们所有人都坚持了下来。

00:29:48.360 --> 00:30:30.590
<v Julie Showalter>You may be asking right now how my father could be so cruel, how he could work young girls like that? Or you may think that I'm exaggerating, that self-pity has magnified our distress. I tell you, this is no exaggeration. And I tell you, my father had no choice. Or that any choice he had was so far in the past that there was no unraveling it. Years later, when we were grown, we caught a glimpse of his guilt, his bitterness over what he had done to us. "I couldn't afford n------," he told my sister Billie. "So I had daughters."
<v Julie Showalter>你现在可能在问，我父亲怎么能如此残忍，他怎么能让年幼的女儿干那种活？或者你可能认为我在夸大其词，认为自怜放大了我们的痛苦。我告诉你，这绝无夸大。而且我告诉你，我父亲别无选择。或者说，他所做的任何选择都已经是在太遥远的过去，已经无法挽回了。多年后，当我们长大成人，我们窥见了他心中的内疚，他对强加给我们的一切所感到的痛苦。“我雇不起黑人劳工，”他对比莉妹妹说，“所以我只能生女儿。”

00:30:30.590 --> 00:30:52.530
<v Julie Showalter>At 6 o'clock, we rebuilt the chute. We opened the doors, and the 6,000 remaining turkeys, the sun now low in the sky behind them, walked through to the pen. We cleaned up. We ate supper. And we went to bed. That's the day we had before the night 3,000 turkeys died.
<v Julie Showalter>6点钟，我们重建了通道。我们打开门，剩下的6000只火鸡，在落日的余晖中，走进了围栏。我们打扫了现场。吃了晚饭。然后上床睡觉。这就是在那三千只火鸡死掉的那晚之前我们所经历的一天。

00:30:52.530 --> 00:31:10.750
<v Julie Showalter>At midnight, Mother woke us up. "We have to get to the pen. Daddy needs us." We had been too exhausted to hear the storm. We ran out in the driving rain. Flashes of lightning showed Daddy picking up turkeys and throwing them, one after the other.
<v Julie Showalter>午夜时分，妈妈叫醒了我们。“我们得去围栏那边。爸爸需要我们。”我们太累了，没能听到暴风雨的声音。我们冒着倾盆大雨跑了出去。闪电照亮了正在抓起火鸡并把它们一只接一只扔开的爸爸。

00:31:10.750 --> 00:31:33.300
<v Julie Showalter>When people learn I grew up on a turkey farm, they invariably ask, "Is it true? Are they really so stupid that they open their mouths in the rain, look up at the sky, and drown?" The answer is yes, some of them do that. They are that stupid. But that's not how 3,000 die in one night.
<v Julie Showalter>当人们知道我在火鸡养殖场长大时，他们总是会问：“是真的吗？它们真的笨到会在雨中张开嘴，仰望天空，然后淹死吗？”答案是肯定的，有些确实会那样。它们就是那么笨。但这就是为什么一晚会死掉3000只的原因。

00:31:33.300 --> 00:31:48.010
<v Julie Showalter>They die because they are scared, and they huddle together in their fear. They climb on top of each other, trying to get close, to find protection in the mass of bodies. And they suffocate.
<v Julie Showalter>它们死是因为它们害怕，在恐惧中它们挤在一起。它们爬到彼此身上，试图靠近，试图在肉堆中寻求保护。然后它们窒息了。

00:31:48.010 --> 00:32:01.050
<v Julie Showalter>We called it piling. It wasn't unusual for a loud noise to cause a pile in the brooder house. If there wasn't someone to pull them off each other, 50 could die because someone slammed a door.
<v Julie Showalter>我们称之为“堆叠”。噪音导致育雏舍里的火鸡堆叠起来并不罕见。如果没有人把它们扒开，50只火鸡可能会因为有人猛关一扇门而死掉。

00:32:01.050 --> 00:32:19.720
<v Julie Showalter>But this was worse than any pile we'd seen, turkeys who'd never spent a night outdoors panicked by thunder, lightning, and rain in sheets. All we could do is pull them out of the pile and throw them away from it. They would run back, still seeking the comfort of the group.
<v Julie Showalter>但这比我们见过的任何堆叠都更严重，那些从未在户外过夜的火鸡被雷声、闪电和倾盆大雨吓坏了。我们唯一能做的就是把它们从堆里拉出来，扔到远处。它们又会跑回来，依然寻找群体带来的安慰。

00:32:19.720 --> 00:32:36.080
<v Julie Showalter>After a while standing in mud, grabbing soaked turkeys, throwing them, grabbing more, you don't know if the ones you are throwing are dead or alive. You don't care. Maybe we saved some.
<v Julie Showalter>在泥泞中站了一会儿，抓起被淋透的火鸡，扔掉，再抓更多，你已经分不清你扔的是死的还是活的了。你根本不在乎。也许我们救下了一些。

00:32:36.080 --> 00:33:16.310
<v Julie Showalter>The next day, the sky was cloudless, and the sun bore down on us again. We picked up dead turkeys, throwing them onto the back of a flatbed truck. Daddy drove the truck into a field far from the house. He poured gasoline on them and struck a match. They burned for days.
<v Julie Showalter>第二天，天空万里无云，太阳再次炙烤着我们。我们捡起死去的火鸡，扔到平板卡车的车厢后。爸爸把卡车开到远离房子的田野里。他往上面倒了汽油，划了一根火柴。它们燃烧了几天几夜。

00:33:16.310 --> 00:33:40.020
<v Ira Glass>Well, Julie Showalter lives in Burr Ridge, Illinois, and says that she still does eat turkey. Well, twice a year, she eats it. And this is the special Poultry Slam edition of Your Radio Playhouse, coming to you from WBEZ in Chicago. Stay with us, won't you?
<v Ira Glass>朱莉·肖沃尔特现在住在伊利诺伊州的伯尔里奇，她说她仍然吃火鸡。嗯，一年吃两次。这就是《你的广播剧场》特别的“家禽大满贯”版本，从芝加哥的WBEZ为您播出。请继续关注我们，好吗？

00:33:40.020 --> 00:34:08.450
<v Luis Rodriguez>This is Luis Rodriguez. And I'm going to read my poem, "The Rooster Who Thought it Was a Dog." Echo Park mornings came on the wings of a rooster's gnawing squawk. This noise, unfortunately, also brought in the afternoon, evenings, and most hours of the day. The rooster had no sense of time, nor any desire to commit to one. He'd cock-a-doodle whenever he had the notion.
<v Luis Rodriguez>我是路易斯·罗德里格斯。我将朗读我的诗《那只自以为是狗的公鸡》。回声公园的早晨是在公鸡刺耳的叫声中降临的。不幸的是，这种噪音也把下午、晚上以及一天中的大部分时间都搅乱了。这只公鸡没有时间观念，也不想遵守任何时间。它只要有兴致就会喔喔叫。

00:34:08.450 --> 00:34:31.990
<v Luis Rodriguez>For late sleepers, day sleepers, or your plain, ordinary, run of the mill night sleepers, annoyance had this rooster's beak. It was enough to drive one crazy. Often, I open my back window that faced the alley just across from the backyard where the rooster made his home. "Shut up, or I'll blow your stinking brains out," I'd yell. Great communication technique.
<v Luis Rodriguez>对于那些起得晚的人、白天睡觉的人，或者普通的夜猫子来说，这只公鸡的喙简直是烦人的源头。这足以让人发疯。我经常打开面对小巷的后窗，而那小巷对面就是公鸡安家的后院。“闭嘴，不然我就打爆你的臭脑袋，”我会大喊。多好的沟通技巧。

00:34:31.990 --> 00:34:48.490
<v Luis Rodriguez>It worked on the brats next door, but the rooster never flinched. With calm aplomb, it continued to squawk. For one thing, the rooster never gave out a bona fide cock-a-doodle. It sort of shouted it out.
<v Luis Rodriguez>这招对隔壁的熊孩子管用，但那只公鸡却从未退缩。它依然泰然自若地继续啼叫。首先，那只公鸡从来没发出过正宗的喔喔声。它更像是喊出来的。

00:34:48.490 --> 00:35:08.110
<v Luis Rodriguez>It happened that the rooster lived with three dogs, a German Shepherd and two mutts. The dogs barked through their existence. They barked at everything in sight. I finally concluded that rooster thought it was a dog. Somehow, I didn't mind the dogs barking. But when a rooster barks, that's murder.
<v Luis Rodriguez>碰巧这只公鸡和三条狗住在一起，一条德国牧羊犬和两只杂种狗。这些狗的一生都在叫。它们对着视野里的一切东西叫。我最后得出结论，那只公鸡以为自己是条狗。不知怎么的，我不介意狗叫。但当一只公鸡叫的时候，那简直是谋杀。

00:35:08.110 --> 00:35:25.660
<v Luis Rodriguez>In fact, I often saw it running alongside the dogs as they raced across the dirt yard, barking at passing cars or people. If the dogs went left, the rooster went left. They'd go right, and dang if that rooster didn't go right as well. Now I don't know if this is a regular condition for roosters.
<v Luis Rodriguez>事实上，我经常看到它和狗一起跑，当它们穿过土院子，对着经过的汽车或行人狂吠时。如果狗向左跑，公鸡也向左跑。它们向右跑，该死的，那只公鸡也会向右跑。现在我不知道这对于公鸡来说是不是一种正常状态。

00:35:25.660 --> 00:35:41.260
<v Luis Rodriguez>I thought I had a story for the Weekly World News. I could see it now. "The Rooster Who Thinks it's a Dog." Who knows what rooster dementia we had here? And whether the rooster chased cats up trees or pissed on fire hydrants, this wasn't clear.
<v Luis Rodriguez>我以为我能给《世界新闻周刊》提供一个故事。我已经能想象到了。“那只自以为是狗的公鸡”。谁知道我们这里出了什么公鸡痴呆症？至于这只公鸡是会追着猫爬树，还是会在消防栓上撒尿，这就不得而知了。

00:35:41.260 --> 00:36:01.880
<v Luis Rodriguez>But once I grasped the heart of the matter, I began to see the rooster in another light. I felt sorry for this fowl with an identity problem. And I wondered how it must react when its owners threw chicken bones to the dogs. Would it nibble on the remains of its favorite hen? I shuddered at the thought.
<v Luis Rodriguez>但一旦我掌握了事情的真相，我开始以另一种眼光看待这只公鸡。我为这只患有身份认同障碍的禽类感到难过。我心想，当主人把鸡骨头扔给狗时，它会作何反应？它会啃食它最喜欢的母鸡的残骸吗？一想到这我不禁打了个寒颤。

00:36:01.880 --> 00:36:17.720
<v Luis Rodriguez>Yet despite the revelation of the rooster's bark, the problem of sleep didn't end. Then one day, a new neighbor, a young lady, who often drank herself to bliss, got a gun and blew the rooster away. She became somewhat of a local hero.
<v Luis Rodriguez>然而，尽管揭开了公鸡叫声的谜底，睡眠问题依然没有解决。后来有一天，一位经常喝得酩酊大醉的新邻居，一位年轻女士，拿了一把枪，把那只公鸡轰飞了。她成了某种当地英雄。

00:36:17.720 --> 00:36:27.470
<v Luis Rodriguez>I must say, though, it was an unfitting end for the bird. But I suppose one can tolerate barking dogs. But barking roosters? That's another matter altogether.
<v Luis Rodriguez>我必须说，这对那只鸟来说是个不合适的结局。但我认为人们可以容忍吠叫的狗。但吠叫的公鸡？那是完全另一回事了。

